// Tuist tailnet ACLs.
//
// Source of truth for who-can-talk-to-what on the Tuist tailnet.
// The file lives in the repo so changes go through code review,
// not via someone's browser session.
//
// HOW IT REACHES THE TAILNET: today, by hand. Paste this file into
// the Tailscale admin console under "Access controls". Nothing in
// CI applies it, so a merged change to this file has NO effect
// until someone does that, a fact worth remembering when a fresh
// grant appears not to work.
//
// Manual is a DECISION, not a platform limitation, and not an
// oversight to go fix. Tailscale does expose the policy file over
// its API (`GET`/`POST /api/v2/tailnet/<tailnet>/acl`, accepting
// HuJSON and honouring `If-Match` for optimistic concurrency, plus
// an `/acl/validate` endpoint), and ships an official GitHub Action,
// `tailscale/gitops-acl-action`, that runs `action: test` on pull
// requests and `action: apply` on merge.
//
// We are not using it yet, because the `policy_file` scope is
// whole-file: there is no way to authorise a credential for "may
// edit the staging grants" and nothing else. A CI identity able to
// apply this file could therefore add a
// `tailscale.com/cap/kubernetes` grant on
// `tag:tuist-k8s-production` handing its holder
// `tuist-production-write`, exactly the elevation the JIT flow
// exists to gate behind a stated intent, a TTL and a second human.
// Automating the paste would put that capability behind "can land a
// commit on main" instead. The `tests` block below cannot backstop
// it either: Tailscale's test engine does not resolve `autogroup:*`,
// so the human grants are precisely the ones an automated `apply`
// would validate the least.
//
// Revisit when both halves have an answer: federated identity
// (GitHub Actions OIDC, so no long-lived secret, see
// https://tailscale.com/kb/1581/workload-identity-federation) for
// the credential, and some way to keep production-tier grants off
// the automated path. Until then, a human pastes it.
//
// Note the OAuth client we already have, used by tuist-ops's
// TuistOps.JIT.TailscaleClient, is deliberately `users:read` only
// and cannot write this file.
//
// There is no catch-all grant. Every path on the tailnet is one of the
// grants below; a flow that is not listed is denied. When something stops
// working after a change here, the missing grant is the first suspect.
//
// Tag taxonomy:
//
//   tag:tuist-mgmt           The management cluster's single Talos VM
//                            (infra/k8s/mgmt/tailscale.yaml). Operators
//                            reach talosd on it at tcp:50000. It
//                            dials nothing and advertises no routes.
//   tag:tuist-macmini-<env>  Mac minis in a specific env's macOS
//                            fleet, plus the xcresult-processor Tart
//                            VMs that share the fleet's credential.
//                            Joined via infra/macos-host-bootstrap;
//                            the env suffix is set by the operator
//                            flag --tailscale-tags, which the chart
//                            wires from macosFleet.tailscale.tags
//                            per env values file. Today every Mac the
//                            env's operator bootstraps carries it:
//                            the xcresult fleet, the runner fleet,
//                            the image builders and the BER1 rack.
//                            Each env joins with its own OAuth client
//                            scoped to this tag, never a pre-auth key,
//                            which Tailscale caps at 90 days and which
//                            took the xcresult queue offline when it
//                            lapsed (2026-08-12). Tags declared here
//                            are what an OAuth-minted key applies, so
//                            a tag removed from tagOwners breaks every
//                            subsequent join.
//   tag:tuist-runner-host-<env>
//                            Mac minis that run customer runner VMs or
//                            bake VM images (runnersFleet, rackFleet,
//                            buildersFleet). Declared ahead of the
//                            operator change that applies it; until
//                            then no device carries it and its grants
//                            are inert. It is tag:tuist-macmini-<env>
//                            without the database ports: a runner
//                            host NATs its guests' traffic to its own
//                            tailnet address, so whatever the host's
//                            tag can reach, a guest running customer
//                            code is one firewall rule away from.
//                            Minted by its own OAuth client per env,
//                            so a runner host's credential cannot
//                            join a device with database access.
//   tag:tuist-k8s-<env>      Tailscale K8s operator, the cluster-side
//                            subnet-router Connector, the egress and
//                            ingress proxies it provisions, scoped to
//                            one env's cluster. Each env runs its own
//                            operator instance with its own OAuth
//                            client, so a leaked staging credential
//                            can't enroll a device under a prod tag.
//   tag:tuist-ops            Reserved for human operators' devices.
//                            No device carries it today: people's
//                            laptops are untagged user devices, and
//                            the human grants below use autogroups.
//                            Env-agnostic deliberately: humans manage
//                            all envs, the tag-per-env split is for
//                            credential isolation, not human access
//                            boundaries.
//   tag:tuist-rack-edge      A rack's edge node, which relays its
//                            switches' management traffic to the
//                            Omada controller on the tailnet,
//                            translated to its own address
//                            (infra/helm/rack-edge).
//                            Joins from its install stick with a
//                            single-use key minted from the
//                            TAILSCALE_RACK_NODES OAuth client,
//                            whose tags must own this tag.
//   tag:tuist-rack-node      A rack's other Linux nodes (services,
//                            storage). They join from a netboot
//                            install with a single-use key the
//                            operator mints from the same OAuth
//                            client, whose tag:tuist-rack-edge
//                            owns this tag.

{
	// Human access groups for the Kubernetes API server proxy.
	//
	// Identity for the human grants below is sourced from the tailnet
	// role (Owner/Admin/Member, set in the Tailscale admin console
	// Users page) via Tailscale's built-in autogroups:
	//   autogroup:owner  + autogroup:admin   -> cluster admins, and
	//                                           the infrastructure
	//                                           planes (Talos, MDM,
	//                                           rack SSH, Omada)
	//   autogroup:member                     -> engineers
	// autogroup:admin does not include the Owner role, so every
	// admin-only grant lists autogroup:owner beside it.
	// No email lists are kept in this file; promoting someone in
	// the console flips their access on the next session.
	//
	// For CANARY and PRODUCTION this document is the **view-tier**
	// policy only. Elevated access (writes) there flows through a
	// separate Pomerium gateway that gates per-request on a Tuist DB
	// flag, with no Tailscale policy mutation. So there are no
	// "break-glass" groups declared here, and the ACL itself is
	// humans-only, edited through code review.
	//
	// STAGING is different: its write tier is standing access rather
	// than a per-request elevation, so there is nothing left for a
	// per-request gateway to decide and staging kubectl runs over
	// the tailnet instead. That makes the
	// `tailscale.com/cap/kubernetes` grants further down the
	// authoritative tier decision for staging, the only place in
	// this file that hands out an `edit`-tier group.
	"groups": {},

	"tagOwners": {
		"tag:tuist-mgmt":                    ["autogroup:admin"],
		"tag:tuist-ops":                     ["autogroup:admin"],
		"tag:tuist-k8s-staging":             ["autogroup:admin"],
		"tag:tuist-k8s-canary":              ["autogroup:admin"],
		"tag:tuist-k8s-production":          ["autogroup:admin"],
		"tag:tuist-macmini-staging":         ["autogroup:admin"],
		"tag:tuist-macmini-canary":          ["autogroup:admin"],
		"tag:tuist-macmini-production":      ["autogroup:admin"],
		// Admin-owned only, not owned by tag:tuist-macmini-<env>: each env's
		// runner hosts join through an OAuth client scoped to this tag alone.
		"tag:tuist-runner-host-staging":     ["autogroup:admin"],
		"tag:tuist-runner-host-canary":      ["autogroup:admin"],
		"tag:tuist-runner-host-production":  ["autogroup:admin"],
		"tag:tuist-rack-edge":               ["autogroup:admin"],
		"tag:tuist-rack-node":               ["autogroup:admin", "tag:tuist-rack-edge"],
	},

	"grants": [
		// ---------------------------------------------------------------
		// Cluster -> Mac minis.
		// ---------------------------------------------------------------

		// Per-env cluster -> matching-env Mac mini fleet, through the
		// macmini-egress ProxyGroup, which carries the cluster's tag. The
		// ports are exactly the ones the per-machine egress Service fronts
		// (infra/cluster-api-provider-tuist/controllers/macos/hostagent.go,
		// reconcileEgressService):
		//   :22         tart-kubelet drift updates. The CAPI controller
		//               rolls host config to already-bootstrapped minis
		//               over the tailnet because a running runner mini
		//               filters inbound :22 on its public interface. Also
		//               `tailscale ssh admin@<vm>` into the xcresult VMs
		//               (see the ssh block).
		//   :8080       controller-runtime metrics on tart-kubelet, scraped
		//               by alloy-metrics.
		//   :9091       PromEx on the xcresult-processor Tart VM, fronted by
		//               tart-kubelet's host-side forwarder. alloy-metrics
		//               scrapes it on `*-macos-fleet` hosts only
		//               (infra/helm/k8s-monitoring/values.yaml).
		//   :9100       node_exporter on the Mac mini host.
		//   :5900-5901  the dashboard's VNC relay, one port per guest the
		//               host runs (DashboardVNCRelayPort in
		//               scalewayapplesiliconmachine_controller.go). The
		//               server dials it through the same egress Service
		//               (server/lib/tuist/runners/interactive_sessions.ex).
		//               Two guests is Apple's cap, hence 5901.
		//
		// The grants are per env pair, so a staging cluster cannot reach a
		// production mini at the tailnet layer, independently of which
		// egress Services each env's controller happens to render.
		{
			"src": ["tag:tuist-k8s-staging"],
			"dst": ["tag:tuist-macmini-staging"],
			"ip":  ["tcp:22", "tcp:8080", "tcp:9091", "tcp:9100", "tcp:5900-5901"],
		},
		{
			"src": ["tag:tuist-k8s-canary"],
			"dst": ["tag:tuist-macmini-canary"],
			"ip":  ["tcp:22", "tcp:8080", "tcp:9091", "tcp:9100", "tcp:5900-5901"],
		},
		{
			"src": ["tag:tuist-k8s-production"],
			"dst": ["tag:tuist-macmini-production"],
			"ip":  ["tcp:22", "tcp:8080", "tcp:9091", "tcp:9100", "tcp:5900-5901"],
		},

		// The same path to runner hosts, minus :9091: runner and builder
		// hosts run no guest metrics endpoint, and alloy-metrics filters the
		// pod-metrics port to the xcresult fleet.
		{
			"src": ["tag:tuist-k8s-staging"],
			"dst": ["tag:tuist-runner-host-staging"],
			"ip":  ["tcp:22", "tcp:8080", "tcp:9100", "tcp:5900-5901"],
		},
		{
			"src": ["tag:tuist-k8s-canary"],
			"dst": ["tag:tuist-runner-host-canary"],
			"ip":  ["tcp:22", "tcp:8080", "tcp:9100", "tcp:5900-5901"],
		},
		{
			"src": ["tag:tuist-k8s-production"],
			"dst": ["tag:tuist-runner-host-production"],
			"ip":  ["tcp:22", "tcp:8080", "tcp:9100", "tcp:5900-5901"],
		},

		// ---------------------------------------------------------------
		// Mac minis -> cluster.
		// ---------------------------------------------------------------

		// Same-env Mac mini fleet -> the cluster's tailnet-exposed Services.
		// Each is a Tailscale operator proxy registered under
		// `tag:tuist-k8s-<env>`, because the operator's OAuth client can
		// only mint that tag, so the dst is wider than the proxies alone.
		//   :5432  CNPG pooler proxy (postgresql-cnpg-pooler.yaml), for the
		//          xcresult-processor VMs.
		//   :8123  the ClickHouse proxy (clickhouse-managed.yaml). The
		//          xcresult processor writes the test tables and is the one
		//          writer not on the pod network, so it reaches the database
		//          this way or not at all. Without this port the VM cannot
		//          connect, which is silent: its logs reach neither kubectl
		//          nor Loki.
		//   :4317  the Alloy receiver's OTLP gRPC intake, for the xcresult
		//          VMs' spans (TUIST_OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT in
		//          values-managed-production.yaml).
		//   :3100  the Alloy receiver's loki.source.api. Both the Mac mini
		//          HOST (infra/macos-log-shipper, macosFleet.hostLogs.url)
		//          and the xcresult VMs (TUIST_LOKI_URL) push logs here. The
		//          host's launchd logs cannot be tailed from the cluster at
		//          all, because a Pod on a macOS Node IS a VM.
		//
		// :443 is gone. It carried the tailnet OCI registry for VM images,
		// which was removed in #12535; images are pulled from and pushed to
		// GHCR over the public internet.
		//
		// 5432 and 8123 are the reason runner hosts get their own tag: the
		// xcresult fleet needs them, a host running customer build code does
		// not.
		{
			"src": ["tag:tuist-macmini-staging"],
			"dst": ["tag:tuist-k8s-staging"],
			"ip":  ["tcp:5432", "tcp:8123", "tcp:4317", "tcp:3100"],
		},
		{
			"src": ["tag:tuist-macmini-canary"],
			"dst": ["tag:tuist-k8s-canary"],
			"ip":  ["tcp:5432", "tcp:8123", "tcp:4317", "tcp:3100"],
		},
		{
			"src": ["tag:tuist-macmini-production"],
			"dst": ["tag:tuist-k8s-production"],
			"ip":  ["tcp:5432", "tcp:8123", "tcp:4317", "tcp:3100"],
		},
		{
			// The in-cluster ClickHouse as a Tailscale Service, for when it is
			// served by the `tailnet-ingress` ProxyGroup rather than a
			// standalone proxy (clickhouse.managed.tailscale.proxyGroup, empty
			// in every env today, so this grant is currently unused). A
			// Service is addressed by its own VIP, which the tag grant above
			// does not cover.
			"src": ["tag:tuist-macmini-production"],
			"dst": ["svc:tuist-clickhouse-production"],
			"ip":  ["tcp:8123"],
		},

		// Runner hosts -> the same-env Alloy receiver, for host logs only.
		// That is the one thing a runner host dials over the tailnet
		// (macosFleet.hostLogs.url, infra/macos-log-shipper).
		{
			"src": ["tag:tuist-runner-host-staging"],
			"dst": ["tag:tuist-k8s-staging"],
			"ip":  ["tcp:3100"],
		},
		{
			"src": ["tag:tuist-runner-host-canary"],
			"dst": ["tag:tuist-k8s-canary"],
			"ip":  ["tcp:3100"],
		},
		{
			"src": ["tag:tuist-runner-host-production"],
			"dst": ["tag:tuist-k8s-production"],
			"ip":  ["tcp:3100"],
		},

		// Staging Mac minis -> the staging cluster's Service CIDR, through
		// the cluster-side subnet-router Connector
		// (infra/helm/tailscale-operator/values-staging.yaml advertises
		// 10.128.0.0/12; infra/k8s/clusters/cluster-staging.yaml is the
		// source of truth for the CIDR). This is the macOS runner-cache
		// path: Tart runner VMs on the minis dial the account's private
		// Kura node at its ClusterIP on 4000, plus cluster DNS on 53
		// (kube-dns ClusterIP lives in the same range) so the VM's
		// /etc/resolver entry can resolve `*.svc.cluster.local` names. The
		// host NATs that VM traffic to its own tailnet address
		// (tuist-pf-vmnat in infra/macos-host-bootstrap), so the source is
		// the host's tag. Subnet-routed destinations are matched by CIDR,
		// not by the router's tag, hence the literal prefix in dst.
		// Per-account isolation is enforced at the Kura app layer.
		//
		// Staging only: all envs share this Service CIDR, so the CIDR-dst
		// grant can't distinguish envs; it points at whichever env's
		// Connector advertises the route. Production's macmini-egress
		// ProxyGroup accepts routes and so installs this one too; no grant
		// lets tag:tuist-k8s-production use it (see the deny test below).
		//
		// The macmini-staging entry stays until staging's runner hosts move
		// to tag:tuist-runner-host-staging; the xcresult fleet does not use
		// this path.
		{
			"src": ["tag:tuist-macmini-staging", "tag:tuist-runner-host-staging"],
			"dst": ["10.128.0.0/12"],
			"ip":  ["tcp:4000", "tcp:53", "udp:53"],
		},

		// ---------------------------------------------------------------
		// Cluster -> cluster.
		// ---------------------------------------------------------------

		// Pomerium in every env -> tuist-ops in production, on tcp:80.
		// tuist-ops runs only in the production cluster and is exposed as
		// `ops` (infra/helm/tuist-ops/templates/service.yaml, port 80). Each
		// env's Pomerium reaches it through the `tuist-ops-egress` Service
		// (infra/helm/pomerium/templates/tailscale-egress.yaml), a standalone
		// egress proxy tagged with that env's own cluster tag, for:
		//   - kube-impersonator's per-request /api/v1/policy lookup
		//     (kube-impersonator/main.go POLICY_URL), canary and production
		//     today, staging as a cold fallback;
		//   - production Pomerium's ops.tuist.dev route, Slack webhooks
		//     included (infra/helm/pomerium/values-production.yaml).
		// This is the one cross-env grant in the file. The dst tag is the
		// whole production cluster tag on :80; narrowing it needs a
		// dedicated tag on the ops Service, which the production operator's
		// OAuth client would have to be allowed to mint.
		{
			"src": ["tag:tuist-k8s-staging", "tag:tuist-k8s-canary", "tag:tuist-k8s-production"],
			"dst": ["tag:tuist-k8s-production"],
			"ip":  ["tcp:80"],
		},

		// ---------------------------------------------------------------
		// BER1 rack.
		// ---------------------------------------------------------------

		// Production cluster -> the BER1 rack's Mac minis, on SSH only.
		//
		// This is the FIRST-DIAL path for rack-owned Mac minis
		// (RackAppleSiliconMachine). A rented mini has a public IP the
		// operator can reach before anything is installed on it; a mini in a
		// rack we own does not, and it is not on the tailnet yet either:
		// bootstrap is what puts it there. So the operator reaches it at its
		// address on the rack's machines segment, which both of the rack's
		// edges advertise (infra/helm/rack-edge), through the per-host
		// `rack-<host>` egress Service on the macmini-egress ProxyGroup.
		//
		// Routing it separately from the host's OWN tailnet identity is
		// deliberate. Bootstrap stops and replaces tailscaled to install the
		// operator's pinned build; over a session transported by the host's
		// own tailnet identity that would drop the tunnel it is riding, which
		// is exactly why the drift loop's tailnet fallback has to set
		// SkipTailscaleInstall. Through a separate router the session
		// survives, so a rack host can be fully bootstrapped in one pass.
		//
		// tcp:22 only: everything else a mini serves (node_exporter,
		// tart-kubelet, the VNC relay) is reached over the per-machine egress
		// Service once the host has joined the tailnet, the same as for a
		// rented one.
		//
		// One /32 per mini, matching exactly what the edges advertise
		// (infra/helm/rack-edge/sites/ber1/tailnet-routes.sh, rendered from
		// infra/rack-switch-fleet/sites/ber1.json, and the hosts' addresses
		// in rackFleet.hosts in values-managed-production.yaml): b01-b09 at
		// 10.10.0.101-10.10.0.109. Not the segment's /24, although the
		// edges' routes are auto-approved inside it: the segment also holds
		// the edges (10.10.0.2, .3) and ber1-store-a (10.10.0.11), and a
		// /24 here would hand SSH to any of them the moment an edge
		// advertised it. Racking a mini means adding its /32 here as well as
		// to its RackHost's `spec.address`.
		{
			"src": ["tag:tuist-k8s-production"],
			"dst": [
				"10.10.0.101/32",
				"10.10.0.102/32",
				"10.10.0.103/32",
				"10.10.0.104/32",
				"10.10.0.105/32",
				"10.10.0.106/32",
				"10.10.0.107/32",
				"10.10.0.108/32",
				"10.10.0.109/32",
			],
			"ip":  ["tcp:22"],
		},

		// Production cluster -> ber1-pdu-b's REST API, which the operator adopts
		// the card through and switches the rack Macs' outlets through (the
		// RackPDU ber1-pdu-b), through the macmini-egress ProxyGroup. The PDU
		// sits on the rack's management LAN; the edge holding the management
		// address advertises it as a /32 and forwards to it with SNAT. HTTPS
		// only.
		{
			"src": ["tag:tuist-k8s-production"],
			"dst": ["192.168.0.16/32"],
			"ip":  ["tcp:443"],
		},

		// Production cluster -> ber1-ats-1 and ber1-ats-3's REST API, which the
		// operator adopts each transfer switch's card through and reads its
		// sources through every minute (the RackATS objects), through the
		// macmini-egress ProxyGroup. Same path as the PDU above. HTTPS only.
		{
			"src": ["tag:tuist-k8s-production"],
			"dst": ["192.168.0.14/32", "192.168.0.15/32"],
			"ip":  ["tcp:443"],
		},

		// Production cluster -> the BER1 rack's Linux nodes. SSH: the operator
		// joins each one over its own tailnet address and keeps it converged
		// (RackLinuxMachine), through the macmini-egress ProxyGroup. 10250: the
		// API server reaches each node's kubelet (logs, exec, port-forward)
		// through the node's own kubelet proxy Pod, which the operator runs.
		{
			"src": ["tag:tuist-k8s-production"],
			"dst": ["tag:tuist-rack-edge", "tag:tuist-rack-node"],
			"ip":  ["tcp:22", "tcp:10250"],
		},

		// Production cluster -> the Kura peer plane on BER1's storage nodes.
		// The rack's cache replicates with each account's other regions, which
		// pull from it over the account's mTLS peer protocol. Nothing in the
		// cluster routes to a rack pod, so they dial the instance's egress
		// Service on the macmini-egress ProxyGroup, which lands on the node's
		// :7443, where the kura-controller's node-local demux forwards by SNI
		// to the pod (infra/kura-controller/AGENTS.md, "Node-local pod
		// networks"). The account CA is the authentication; this grant is
		// reachability. The edges hold no cache.
		{
			"src": ["tag:tuist-k8s-production"],
			"dst": ["tag:tuist-rack-node"],
			"ip":  ["tcp:7443"],
		},

		// The BER1 switches -> the Omada controller in the production cluster,
		// on its device ports only. The switches are not on the tailnet: their
		// traffic reaches it through the rack's edge node, translated to the
		// edge node's own address, so the source here is the edge node's tag
		// and the management prefix is not advertised or routed. The
		// destination is the production operator's tag, which the
		// controller's exposed Service carries. 29811-29817 are the device
		// management, adoption, upgrade and monitoring channels; 8044 firmware
		// upgrades; 29810 and 19810 discovery and management over UDP.
		{
			"src": ["tag:tuist-rack-edge"],
			"dst": ["tag:tuist-k8s-production"],
			"ip":  ["tcp:29811-29817", "tcp:8044", "udp:29810", "udp:19810"],
		},

		// BER1's Linux nodes -> the production Alloy receiver: pod logs to its
		// loki.source.api (3100) and node-local Kura metrics to its
		// prometheus.receive_http (9009), both from alloy-rack in
		// infra/helm/k8s-monitoring, and Kura's traces to its OTLP HTTP
		// intake (4318, TUIST_KURA_NODE_LOCAL_OTLP_TRACES_ENDPOINT). A rack
		// node has no route to cluster Services.
		{
			"src": ["tag:tuist-rack-edge", "tag:tuist-rack-node"],
			"dst": ["tag:tuist-k8s-production"],
			"ip":  ["tcp:3100", "tcp:9009", "tcp:4318"],
		},

		// ---------------------------------------------------------------
		// Humans.
		// ---------------------------------------------------------------

		// Every member -> their own devices, on any port. Tailscale's default
		// policy carries this grant, and the `check` SSH rule below
		// (autogroup:member -> autogroup:self) needs a network path to be
		// usable at all.
		{
			"src": ["autogroup:member"],
			"dst": ["autogroup:self"],
			"ip":  ["*"],
		},

		// Admins -> talosd on the management cluster's Talos VM. The VM joins
		// the tailnet so operators reach :50000 without a Hetzner firewall
		// allowlist (infra/k8s/mgmt/tailscale.yaml); `talosctl --endpoints
		// tuist-mgmt` is the documented path (infra/k8s/onboarding.md). The
		// Talos API's own mTLS (talosconfig) is the authentication; this
		// grant is reachability. Its :6443 stays public for CI. A single-node
		// cluster has no trustd peers, so :50001 is not granted.
		{
			"src": ["autogroup:owner", "autogroup:admin"],
			"dst": ["tag:tuist-mgmt"],
			"ip":  ["tcp:50000"],
		},

		// Admins -> the MDM's operator APIs in production: nanomdm on 9000
		// (`mdm-api`) and nanodep on 9001 (`mdm-dep`), each a standalone proxy
		// carrying the production cluster tag (infra/helm/mdm, tailnet.tags).
		// Only people call them, with curl, for the APNs certificate and ABM
		// token setup (infra/mdm/README.md); in-cluster callers use the
		// ClusterIP Services. Enrolling Macs use the public mdm.tuist.dev.
		{
			"src": ["autogroup:owner", "autogroup:admin"],
			"dst": ["tag:tuist-k8s-production"],
			"ip":  ["tcp:9000", "tcp:9001"],
		},

		// Admins -> the Omada controller's web UI and Open API in production
		// (`omada-production`, :8043). The rack-switch-fleet tooling
		// (infra/rack-switch-fleet/omada.sh) runs from a laptop against it.
		// The controller's own login is the access control.
		{
			"src": ["autogroup:owner", "autogroup:admin"],
			"dst": ["tag:tuist-k8s-production"],
			"ip":  ["tcp:8043"],
		},

		// Admins -> SSH on the BER1 rack's Linux nodes. The edges' SSH is
		// reachable over the tailnet only (their WAN drops inbound :22,
		// infra/rack-switch-fleet/AGENTS.md "Closed from outside"), and the
		// fleet tooling uses `tuist@ber1-edge-a` as its jump host
		// (infra/rack-switch-fleet/sites/ber1.json, lib/session.sh). The rest
		// of the rack (the other edge on the VRRP link, ber1-store-a, the
		// minis) is reached from the edge over the rack's own LAN, not the
		// tailnet. The nodes authorize the fleet key and
		// rackLinuxFleet.authorizedKeys; that is the authentication.
		{
			"src": ["autogroup:owner", "autogroup:admin"],
			"dst": ["tag:tuist-rack-edge", "tag:tuist-rack-node"],
			"ip":  ["tcp:22"],
		},

		// tag:tuist-ops -> SSH into any Mac mini. No device carries
		// tag:tuist-ops today, so this grant matches nothing: people reach a
		// rented mini on its public address and a rack mini through an edge.
		// It is kept so tagging an ops device is the only step needed.
		{
			"src": ["tag:tuist-ops"],
			"dst": [
				"tag:tuist-macmini-staging",
				"tag:tuist-macmini-canary",
				"tag:tuist-macmini-production",
				"tag:tuist-runner-host-staging",
				"tag:tuist-runner-host-canary",
				"tag:tuist-runner-host-production",
			],
			"ip": ["tcp:22"],
		},

		// Human kubectl to CANARY and PRODUCTION is NOT a tailnet
		// grant. Pomerium per env handles it via Google Workspace
		// OIDC (`kube-<env>.tuist.dev`) + per-request impersonation
		// resolved by tuist-ops's PolicyController, which reads the
		// live JIT elevation row. That decision cannot be expressed
		// in a static ACL, which is exactly why those two envs stay
		// behind the gateway. No `tailscale.com/cap/kubernetes`
		// grants for `tag:tuist-k8s-canary` or
		// `tag:tuist-k8s-production` belong in this file.

		// Human kubectl to STAGING, on the other hand, IS a tailnet
		// grant. Staging's write tier is standing access rather than
		// a per-request elevation
		// (`TuistOps.JIT.Policy.always_write_env?/1`), so there is no
		// per-request lookup left to do and no reason to make anyone
		// re-authenticate to a browser gateway for it. The staging
		// operator runs Tailscale's in-process API server proxy in
		// auth mode (`apiServerProxyConfig.mode: "true"` in
		// infra/helm/tailscale-operator/values-staging.yaml) at
		// `tuist-k8s-staging.taild6d7bb.ts.net`; it WhoIs's the
		// caller and turns the `impersonate.groups` below into the
		// `Impersonate-Group` headers the apiserver binds.
		//
		// The group names are the same ones Pomerium's chart binds to
		// ClusterRoles in
		// infra/helm/pomerium/templates/access-tiers.yaml; that file
		// is load-bearing for BOTH doors into staging, so it must
		// not be deleted along with a future Pomerium teardown.
		//
		// KNOWN GAP vs the gateway path: `autogroup:member` is
		// role-agnostic. Tailscale documents it as every direct
		// member of the tailnet, which includes admin-flavour roles
		// (Auditor, Billing admin, IT admin, Network admin).
		// tuist-ops's PolicyController denies those roles outright.
		// So a non-engineering tailnet role that would get nothing
		// through Pomerium gets `tuist-eng` + `tuist-staging-write`
		// here. Accepted for staging while every member of this
		// tailnet is an engineer. The fix, if that stops being true,
		// is to declare an explicit `group:tuist-eng` in the `groups`
		// block above and use it as `src` instead of the autogroup,
		// at the cost of keeping an email list in this file.
		//
		// Owners and Admins match BOTH grants (they are members too),
		// so they receive `tuist-admins` + `tuist-eng` +
		// `tuist-staging-write`. Harmless: `tuist-admins` and
		// `tuist-eng` both bind to `view`, and the union is the same
		// tier either way.
		//
		// `ip: tcp:443` is what actually reaches the proxy. The dst
		// tag is shared with the cluster's subnet router and egress
		// proxies, so this opens 443 slightly wider than the operator
		// alone, the same caveat recorded for the cluster's exposed
		// Services above.
		{
			"src": ["autogroup:member"],
			"dst": ["tag:tuist-k8s-staging"],
			"ip":  ["tcp:443"],
			"app": {
				"tailscale.com/cap/kubernetes": [
					{"impersonate": {"groups": ["tuist-eng", "tuist-staging-write"]}},
				],
			},
		},
		{
			"src": ["autogroup:owner", "autogroup:admin"],
			"dst": ["tag:tuist-k8s-staging"],
			"ip":  ["tcp:443"],
			"app": {
				"tailscale.com/cap/kubernetes": [
					{"impersonate": {"groups": ["tuist-admins", "tuist-staging-write"]}},
				],
			},
		},

		// Elevated (`edit`) access for CANARY and PRODUCTION does not
		// live here. It flows through the Pomerium gateway (see
		// `infra/helm/pomerium/`), which authenticates callers via
		// Google Workspace OIDC, looks up the active elevation in
		// the tuist-ops DB per request, and injects the
		// Impersonate-Group header that maps to the env's elevated
		// ClusterRole. For those two envs the tailnet ACL stays
		// read-only and static: no runtime mutation, no break-glass
		// groups, and no role-tier branching at this layer (Pomerium
		// owns that decision).
	],

	// Subnet-route auto-approval, so the staging Connector's
	// advertised Service CIDR goes live on `helm upgrade` without a
	// human clicking "approve" in the admin console (the Connector
	// Pod re-advertises on every recreate; manual approval would
	// break the route on each rollout). Scoped to the staging
	// cluster tag. The 100.64.0.0/10 advertisement every env's
	// Connector also makes (the tailscale-operator chart default)
	// predates this section and stays manually approved; it is the
	// tailnet's own range and no grant here targets it.
	//
	// The BER1 edges' routes are auto-approved too, and only inside the
	// rack's machines segment. They advertise one /32 per Mac mini,
	// rendered from the rack's inventory (infra/rack-switch-fleet), so
	// racking a mini changes their routes and hand approval would be a step
	// per mini per edge. Scoped to the segment, the tag can put nothing else
	// into the tailnet's routing table; what is reachable through those
	// routes is decided by the per-host grant above, not by the approval.
	// On the management LAN, only the installed power devices' addresses
	// are approved: ber1-ats-1, ber1-ats-3 and ber1-pdu-b.
	"autoApprovers": {
		"routes": {
			"10.128.0.0/12":   ["tag:tuist-k8s-staging"],
			"10.10.0.0/24":    ["tag:tuist-rack-edge"],
			"192.168.0.14/32": ["tag:tuist-rack-edge"],
			"192.168.0.15/32": ["tag:tuist-rack-edge"],
			"192.168.0.16/32": ["tag:tuist-rack-edge"],
		},
		// Tailscale Services the operator's ingress ProxyGroups advertise
		// (infra/helm/tailscale-operator, `tailnetIngress`). The operator
		// creates the Service when a Kubernetes Service opts in, and its
		// proxies re-advertise it on every restart, so hand approval would
		// take it offline on each rollout.
		"services": {
			"svc:tuist-clickhouse-production": ["tag:tuist-k8s-production"],
		},
	},

	// Tailscale SSH (distinct from "grants"). Only the xcresult-processor
	// Tart VMs run tailscaled with --ssh=true
	// (infra/xcresult-processor-image/tailscale-up.sh); Mac mini hosts and
	// rack nodes join with --ssh=false, so the accept rules below reach the
	// VMs only. They let the cluster-side subnet router
	// `tailscale ssh admin@<vm-tailnet-ip>` into matching-env xcresult VMs:
	// diagnostic shell access when `kubectl logs` / `kubectl exec` against
	// the kubelet endpoint fails (tart-kubelet's Node hostname doesn't
	// resolve from the K8s apiserver's DNS, so the standard debug path is
	// broken on macOS fleets and this is the substitute). The tcp:22 in the
	// cluster -> Mac mini grants is the network half of it.
	//
	// Locked down to the `admin` user only, the bake-time user that owns
	// /opt/tuist and the launchd plist. Explicitly excludes `root` and the
	// broader `autogroup:nonroot` to keep a compromise of the k8s tag
	// (large blast radius: cluster subnet router, every operator pod the
	// Tailscale operator exposes) from being a root-shell pivot.
	// Tagged-device SSH can't use `check` mode (no re-auth flow for a
	// non-human source), so `accept` is the only option and the narrowing
	// happens via `users`. No rule targets tag:tuist-runner-host-<env>:
	// runner hosts and their guests run no Tailscale SSH server. See
	// sshTests below.
	"ssh": [
		{
			"action": "check",
			"src":    ["autogroup:member"],
			"dst":    ["autogroup:self"],
			"users":  ["autogroup:nonroot", "root"],
		},
		{
			"action": "accept",
			"src":    ["tag:tuist-k8s-staging"],
			"dst":    ["tag:tuist-macmini-staging"],
			"users":  ["admin"],
		},
		{
			"action": "accept",
			"src":    ["tag:tuist-k8s-canary"],
			"dst":    ["tag:tuist-macmini-canary"],
			"users":  ["admin"],
		},
		{
			"action": "accept",
			"src":    ["tag:tuist-k8s-production"],
			"dst":    ["tag:tuist-macmini-production"],
			"users":  ["admin"],
		},
	],

	// SSH ACL assertions, enforced on every save in the admin
	// console. The accept lines lock in the `admin`-only narrowing
	// added with #11134's diagnostic grants; the reject lines
	// catch the failure modes that would re-expand blast radius:
	// root SSH (host take-over), cross-env SSH (staging subnet
	// router into production VMs), and Tailscale SSH onto runner
	// hosts.
	"sshTests": [
		{
			"src":    "tag:tuist-k8s-staging",
			"dst":    ["tag:tuist-macmini-staging"],
			"accept": ["admin"],
		},
		{
			"src":    "tag:tuist-k8s-staging",
			"dst":    ["tag:tuist-macmini-staging"],
			"deny":   ["root"],
		},
		{
			"src":    "tag:tuist-k8s-staging",
			"dst":    ["tag:tuist-macmini-production"],
			"deny":   ["admin"],
		},
		{
			"src":    "tag:tuist-k8s-canary",
			"dst":    ["tag:tuist-macmini-canary"],
			"accept": ["admin"],
		},
		{
			"src":    "tag:tuist-k8s-canary",
			"dst":    ["tag:tuist-macmini-canary"],
			"deny":   ["root"],
		},
		{
			"src":    "tag:tuist-k8s-production",
			"dst":    ["tag:tuist-macmini-production"],
			"accept": ["admin"],
		},
		{
			"src":    "tag:tuist-k8s-production",
			"dst":    ["tag:tuist-macmini-production"],
			"deny":   ["root"],
		},
		{
			"src":    "tag:tuist-k8s-production",
			"dst":    ["tag:tuist-runner-host-production"],
			"deny":   ["admin", "root"],
		},
	],

	// Tests run on every save in the console. With no catch-all, the deny
	// assertions are meaningful: they pin the boundaries this file exists
	// to draw (cross-env, Mac minis away from other envs' databases, runner
	// hosts away from every database, nothing but the production cluster
	// into the rack).
	//
	// Note: no test entries with autogroup-based src. Tailscale's test
	// engine doesn't resolve `autogroup:*` as a concrete identity, so the
	// human grants (Talos, MDM, Omada UI, rack SSH, staging kubectl) are
	// exercised only by the live access decisions in the console. Check
	// them by hand after a paste (NOTES.md in the draft lists how).
	"tests": [
		// Cluster -> same-env Mac minis: scrape, drift SSH and VNC relay.
		{
			"src": "tag:tuist-k8s-staging",
			"accept": [
				"tag:tuist-macmini-staging:22",
				"tag:tuist-macmini-staging:8080",
				"tag:tuist-macmini-staging:9091",
				"tag:tuist-macmini-staging:9100",
				"tag:tuist-macmini-staging:5900",
				"tag:tuist-macmini-staging:5901",
				"tag:tuist-runner-host-staging:22",
				"tag:tuist-runner-host-staging:5901",
			],
			"deny": [
				"tag:tuist-macmini-production:22",
				"tag:tuist-macmini-production:9100",
				"tag:tuist-macmini-canary:5900",
				"tag:tuist-runner-host-production:22",
				"tag:tuist-k8s-production:5432",
				"tag:tuist-k8s-production:8123",
				"tag:tuist-k8s-production:9000",
				"tag:tuist-mgmt:50000",
				"10.10.0.101:22",
				"192.168.0.16:443",
				"tag:tuist-rack-edge:22",
			],
		},
		{
			"src": "tag:tuist-k8s-canary",
			"accept": [
				"tag:tuist-macmini-canary:22",
				"tag:tuist-macmini-canary:8080",
				"tag:tuist-macmini-canary:9091",
				"tag:tuist-macmini-canary:9100",
				"tag:tuist-macmini-canary:5900",
				"tag:tuist-macmini-canary:5901",
				"tag:tuist-runner-host-canary:22",
			],
			"deny": [
				"tag:tuist-macmini-production:22",
				"tag:tuist-macmini-staging:22",
				"tag:tuist-k8s-production:5432",
				"10.10.0.101:22",
			],
		},
		{
			"src": "tag:tuist-k8s-production",
			"accept": [
				"tag:tuist-macmini-production:22",
				"tag:tuist-macmini-production:8080",
				"tag:tuist-macmini-production:9091",
				"tag:tuist-macmini-production:9100",
				"tag:tuist-macmini-production:5900",
				"tag:tuist-macmini-production:5901",
				"tag:tuist-runner-host-production:22",
				"tag:tuist-runner-host-production:5900",
			],
			"deny": [
				"tag:tuist-macmini-staging:22",
				"tag:tuist-macmini-canary:9100",
				"tag:tuist-runner-host-production:9091",
				// Production's egress proxies accept staging's Service-CIDR
				// route; nothing may use it.
				"10.128.0.10:4000",
				"10.128.0.10:53",
				"tag:tuist-mgmt:50000",
			],
		},

		// Pomerium in every env -> tuist-ops in production on :80, and
		// nothing else in production from the other envs.
		{
			"src": "tag:tuist-k8s-staging",
			"accept": ["tag:tuist-k8s-production:80"],
			"deny":   ["tag:tuist-k8s-production:3100", "tag:tuist-k8s-production:8043", "tag:tuist-k8s-canary:80"],
		},
		{
			"src": "tag:tuist-k8s-canary",
			"accept": ["tag:tuist-k8s-production:80"],
			"deny":   ["tag:tuist-k8s-production:3100", "tag:tuist-k8s-production:9001"],
		},
		{
			"src": "tag:tuist-k8s-production",
			"accept": ["tag:tuist-k8s-production:80"],
		},

		// Mac minis (xcresult fleet) -> same-env cluster: databases,
		// telemetry and host logs, and not :443 any more. A narrowing that
		// dropped 3100 would silently take the macOS fleet's only queryable
		// output offline, with a healthy-looking Node.
		{
			"src": "tag:tuist-macmini-staging",
			"accept": [
				"tag:tuist-k8s-staging:5432",
				"tag:tuist-k8s-staging:8123",
				"tag:tuist-k8s-staging:4317",
				"tag:tuist-k8s-staging:3100",
				"10.128.0.10:4000",
				"10.128.0.10:53",
			],
			"deny": [
				"tag:tuist-k8s-staging:443",
				"tag:tuist-k8s-staging:80",
				"tag:tuist-k8s-production:5432",
				"tag:tuist-k8s-production:8123",
				"tag:tuist-k8s-production:3100",
				"tag:tuist-k8s-canary:5432",
				"tag:tuist-macmini-staging:22",
				"tag:tuist-macmini-production:22",
				"tag:tuist-mgmt:50000",
				"10.10.0.101:22",
			],
		},
		{
			"src": "tag:tuist-macmini-canary",
			"accept": [
				"tag:tuist-k8s-canary:5432",
				"tag:tuist-k8s-canary:8123",
				"tag:tuist-k8s-canary:4317",
				"tag:tuist-k8s-canary:3100",
			],
			"deny": [
				"tag:tuist-k8s-production:5432",
				"tag:tuist-k8s-staging:5432",
				"10.128.0.10:4000",
			],
		},
		{
			"src": "tag:tuist-macmini-production",
			"accept": [
				"tag:tuist-k8s-production:5432",
				"tag:tuist-k8s-production:8123",
				"tag:tuist-k8s-production:4317",
				"tag:tuist-k8s-production:3100",
			],
			"deny": [
				"tag:tuist-k8s-production:443",
				"tag:tuist-k8s-production:80",
				"tag:tuist-k8s-production:9000",
				"tag:tuist-k8s-production:8043",
				"tag:tuist-k8s-staging:5432",
				"tag:tuist-k8s-canary:8123",
				"10.128.0.10:4000",
				"10.10.0.101:22",
				"10.10.0.109:22",
				"192.168.0.16:443",
				"tag:tuist-rack-edge:22",
				"tag:tuist-rack-node:10250",
				"tag:tuist-rack-node:7443",
				"tag:tuist-macmini-production:22",
			],
		},

		// Runner hosts: host logs to the same-env receiver, the staging
		// runner-cache path, and no database, telemetry or rack port.
		{
			"src": "tag:tuist-runner-host-staging",
			"accept": [
				"tag:tuist-k8s-staging:3100",
				"10.128.0.10:4000",
				"10.128.0.10:53",
			],
			"deny": [
				"tag:tuist-k8s-staging:5432",
				"tag:tuist-k8s-staging:8123",
				"tag:tuist-k8s-staging:4317",
				"tag:tuist-k8s-staging:443",
				"tag:tuist-k8s-production:3100",
				"tag:tuist-macmini-staging:22",
				"tag:tuist-runner-host-staging:5900",
			],
		},
		{
			"src": "tag:tuist-runner-host-canary",
			"accept": ["tag:tuist-k8s-canary:3100"],
			"deny": [
				"tag:tuist-k8s-canary:5432",
				"tag:tuist-k8s-canary:8123",
				"10.128.0.10:4000",
			],
		},
		{
			"src": "tag:tuist-runner-host-production",
			"accept": ["tag:tuist-k8s-production:3100"],
			"deny": [
				"tag:tuist-k8s-production:5432",
				"tag:tuist-k8s-production:8123",
				"tag:tuist-k8s-production:4317",
				"tag:tuist-k8s-production:80",
				"tag:tuist-k8s-production:9000",
				"10.128.0.10:4000",
				"10.10.0.102:22",
				"192.168.0.14:443",
				"tag:tuist-rack-node:22",
				"tag:tuist-macmini-production:22",
			],
		},

		// tag:tuist-ops (no device carries it today) -> SSH on every Mac
		// mini, nothing else.
		{
			"src": "tag:tuist-ops",
			"accept": [
				"tag:tuist-macmini-staging:22",
				"tag:tuist-macmini-canary:22",
				"tag:tuist-macmini-production:22",
				"tag:tuist-runner-host-production:22",
			],
			"deny": [
				"tag:tuist-k8s-production:5432",
				"tag:tuist-rack-edge:22",
			],
		},

		// The BER1 rack and the production cluster: the operator to the rack
		// (every mini's first-dial address, the power cards, the Linux
		// nodes), the switches to the Omada controller, and the rack nodes'
		// telemetry.
		{
			"src": "tag:tuist-k8s-production",
			"accept": [
				"tag:tuist-rack-edge:22",
				"tag:tuist-rack-node:22",
				"tag:tuist-rack-edge:10250",
				"tag:tuist-rack-node:10250",
				"tag:tuist-rack-node:7443",
				"10.10.0.101:22",
				"10.10.0.103:22",
				"10.10.0.104:22",
				"10.10.0.109:22",
				"192.168.0.14:443",
				"192.168.0.15:443",
				"192.168.0.16:443",
			],
			"deny": [
				// The edges' and ber1-store-a's addresses on the machines
				// segment are not granted, even if an edge advertised them.
				"10.10.0.2:22",
